<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("radius") %>
<% radii = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("radius") %>
<% local_radii = radii.reject(&:zero?) %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% rate_exponent = -Math.log2(Lab::Schema.run_defaults.fetch("mutation_rate")).round %>

<%# The recorded result of the finished sweep. Runs are named by arm and seed, never by a
    database id, and the live tables further down re-read the same runs. %>
<% arms = [
     { label: "1", radius: 1, transitions: [[9, 15_560]], floor: "0.951", entropy: "7.89" },
     { label: "2", radius: 2, transitions: [[10, 12_330]], floor: "0.901", entropy: "7.52" },
     { label: "4", radius: 4, transitions: [[10, 5_910], [1, 11_250]], floor: "0.860", entropy: "7.27" },
     { label: "well-mixed", radius: 0, transitions: [[5, 11_030], [1, 12_400]], floor: "0.859", entropy: "7.26" }
   ] %>

<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 3 asks what spatial locality buys, and it asks it twice. A
  neighbourhood radius decides who a cell can interact with: at
  <span class="mono"><%= local_radii.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span> a
  tape only ever meets the tapes inside that Moore neighbourhood, while
  <span class="mono">radius = 0</span> is the well-mixed world — a partner drawn uniformly
  from the whole torus, the infinity arm of the design record. The first half of the
  hypothesis is about speed: a tight neighbourhood shelters a new lineage from the soup
  long enough to take hold, so a local world should reach the first replicator sooner and
  more often than a well-mixed one. The second half is about what happens afterwards:
  locality is supposed to keep a world diverse once a lineage exists, by stopping one
  winner from reaching every cell. This finding answers the first half only.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  One world shape and one parameter moving. A 128×128 torus of 64-byte tapes at the
  engine's default mutation rate, <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span>,
  <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %> seeds per arm and <%= epochs %> epochs per run, with the
  radius taking <span class="mono"><%= radii.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>
  and every other parameter at its default. The dependent variable is the transition epoch
  of DESIGN §1.2 — the first sampled epoch at which <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span>
  falls below 0.6 and stays below it for three further samples. A run that never crosses is
  censored at <%= epochs %> epochs, not zero.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  All 40 runs finished and 6 of them transitioned. The table is the recorded per-arm
  result; the phase diagram and the per-run rows below read the same runs live, and the
  sweep's own page adds the hazard table.
</p>

<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Result per neighbourhood">
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Neighbourhood</th>
        <th class="numeric">Transitioned</th>
        <th>Transition epochs</th>
        <th class="numeric">Median final <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span></th>
        <th class="numeric">Median final <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% arms.each do |arm| %>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono"><%= arm.fetch(:label) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:transitions).size %> of <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %></td>
          <td class="mono">
            <%= safe_join(arm.fetch(:transitions).sort_by(&:last).map { |seed, epoch|
                  safe_join([number_with_delimiter(epoch, delimiter: " "), " (",
                             findings_run_link(experiment_slug: "radius", seed: seed,
                                               params: { "radius" => arm.fetch(:radius) }),
                             ")"])
                }, ", ") %>
          </td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:floor) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:entropy) %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-muted">
  The two medians are taken over the runs of each arm that did <em>not</em> transition — the
  soup the arm sits in when nothing emerges — at the last sampled epoch.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Locality did not speed emergence.</strong> The well-mixed arm, which has no
  locality at all, transitioned as often as the best local arm and sooner than two of them.
  Ordered by how much of the world a cell can reach, the counts run 1, 1, 2, 2 with the
  well-mixed arm at the top of that list rather than the bottom, and the earliest transition
  of the sweep belongs to the widest local arm, not the tightest. The effect the hypothesis
  predicts — a local arm reaching the first replicator sooner or more often than the
  well-mixed one — is not in the data.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>And ten seeds could not have seen it unless it were enormous.</strong> The widest
  spread in the sweep is 2 of 10 against 1 of 10, which is a one-sided Fisher exact
  <span class="mono">p = 0.5</span> — the two arms are not distinguishable at all. With ten
  seeds an arm cannot reach <span class="mono">p &lt; 0.05</span> against another until it
  shows 4 transitions where the other shows none. The hazard section on the sweep's page
  says the same thing on the runs' own time at risk: every arm's 95% interval contains every
  other arm's estimate, from roughly 0.05 to 0.11 events per 10<sup>4</sup> run-epochs, and
  each interval is 30-fold wide in the arms with two events and more than 200-fold wide in
  the arms with one. So this is a negative result about an
  effect of the size the hypothesis imagined, not a demonstration that radius does nothing:
  a factor of two hiding in here would be invisible.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>One transition did not hold.</strong> Five of the six transitioned runs end
  collapsed, with <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> between 0.07 and 0.31 at the last
  epoch. The sixth — the <span class="mono">radius = 2</span> arm's flagged run — falls to a
  floor near 0.11, stays down for about two thousand epochs, then climbs back through the
  0.6 line and ends at 0.91, next to the 0.894 to 0.904 of its arm's seeds that never
  moved. It is a
  genuine crossing by the locked rule and a reminder that a transition in the DESIGN §1.2
  sense is a state a world can leave again.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>What the sweep does show is about the soup, not about emergence.</strong> Among
  the runs that never transitioned, the final <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> falls
  monotonically as a cell's reach widens — 0.951, 0.901, 0.860 across the three local arms —
  and the well-mixed arm sits at 0.859, at the end of that trend rather than apart from it.
  The arms' spreads do not overlap between <span class="mono">radius = 1</span>,
  <span class="mono">radius = 2</span> and the pair above them, and
  <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> orders the arms identically. Wider mixing leaves the
  tapes more alike than a tight neighbourhood does. That is a real, clean effect on the
  pre-emergence soup and nothing more: it is measured on the runs where nothing emerged, and
  the mutation-rate sweep already found a floor of the same kind that moved with its
  parameter while emergence did not.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>A determinism cross-check, for free.</strong> The
  <span class="mono">radius = 1</span> arm is the engine's default radius, so its ten runs
  repeat the parameters of the <%= link_to "mutation-rate", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %>
  sweep's <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span> arm and the
  <%= link_to "world-size", finding_path("world-size-scaling") %> sweep's 128² arm. All ten
  seeds reproduce: the same transition epoch in the one seed that transitions, and the same
  final <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span>, <span class="mono">distinct_tapes</span>,
  <span class="mono">top_share</span> and <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> in every
  one of them, digit for digit, across three experiments run weeks apart.
  <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> agrees wherever all three recorded it, which for the
  mutation-rate arm was only the seed that transitioned. That is the
  guarantee DESIGN §1.1 makes — a run is a function of
  <span class="mono">(params, seed)</span> — checked against itself rather than asserted. It
  is also a caveat on this sweep: that arm is not an independent sample, so the sweep holds
  30 runs of new evidence and 10 of a repeat.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  The second half of the hypothesis is untouched and stays open: whether locality
  <em>stabilises</em> diversity once a replicator exists is a question about the six
  transitioned runs' <span class="mono">distinct_tapes</span> and
  <span class="mono">top_share</span> series after their transition epochs, not about how
  many arms transitioned, and nothing here tests it. Six runs spread over four arms is too
  thin to read that way — the comparison wants several transitions per arm — so it is left
  for the sweep that has them. The hazard section on the
  <%= link_to "sweep's page", experiment_path("radius") %> is the right lens for the speed
  question once more seeds exist: it spends a censored run's whole observed lifetime rather
  than throwing it away, and the way to move those intervals is more seeds or longer runs,
  which is what <%= link_to "mutation rate, long runs", experiment_path("mutation-rate-long") %>
  and the <%= link_to "BFF positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> are for. Until
  the control resolves, an arm that shows nothing here is still evidence about the
  instrument as well as about the world.
</p>
